Thank You professor wolf you've  certainly facilitated my contribution  with that very wide-ranging introduction  to the subject let me begin by drawing  attention to the important word or  dignity or rights in the primary  question for today how can we promote  the internal resources of faith  traditions that affirm the dignity or  rights of all human beings with this  word or the question opens up discussion  about whether or not affirmations by  faith traditions of the dignity of human  beings have provided historically and  should provide ethically considered  justifications for universal attribution  to them of equal rights the theoretical  priority of human dignity to human  rights is central to Christian  theological traditions and I will  conjecture to other religious traditions  which this course encompasses and let us  note in passing that this priority has  been recognized by certain United  Nations documents which explicitly  derive rights from the inherent dignity  of the human person all Christian  theologies rooted in the Bible have  offered understandings of human dignity  signifying that the unique determination  worth ontological and moral status of  human beings generically and  individually and these various  understandings have been more or less  hospitable to conceptualizing political  justice in terms of human rights at the  outset we should observe that all  Christian understandings of human  dignity have drawn on the biblical  doctrine of  Margot D the doctrine that humankind was  originally created in the image of God  and that fallen humanity corrupted by  sin has been his being and will be  restored to God's image in Jesus Christ  enjoying on this doctrine Christian  theologians have proposed that the most  adequate understanding of human dignity  must attend to the manifold and ongoing  relations of humankind and of individual  persons to the triune God the Father Son  and Holy Spirit relations initiated and  sustained by God's own creating  sustaining redeeming and sanctifying  action this common Trinitarian ground  however leaves scope for disagreement on  an issue which bears immediately on the  theological assessment of human rights  namely whether the theologian while  regarding divine human relations as  constitutive of the imago Dei and of  human dignity in its fullest meaning can  also entertain a conception of human  dignity in terms of imminent or  self-standing  human faculties qualities or capacities  such as self-consciousness rationality  practical freedom or social relatedness  may the two accounts both claiming  universality be viewed as complementary  or must they be be viewed as  alternatives this issue has obvious  implications for assessing the language  of human rights for rights in the plural  refer to either natural moral or  positive legal powers to have or to do  something attributable to individual or  to collective human subjects and  defensible against all other subjects  thus rights refer to imminent self  standing human qualities or capacities  and moreover they are inextricably  linked to accounts of human dignity into  of such imminent entities the  theological issue presented by human  rights then is whether a language of  natural and political justice that  deflects attention away from the divine  human relationship should be endorsed in  addition to or in lieu of a language  which keeps attention focused on that  relationship I'm not suggesting that  this issue has been and is today the  only issue of theological importance  concerning human dignity and rights  there are important contemporary  discussions about the meaning of human  nature and natural law natural light  about the social dimensions of  individual freedom and moral reasoning  about the objective relational character  of justice and these discussions have  raised weighty objections to the  prevailing liberal democratic theory of  natural and positive rights criticizing  its individualism its volunteerism  ethical irrational ism subjectivism  false egalitarian ism rather I'm  suggesting that the issue of whether and  how a theological exploitation of human  dignity should put God's Trinitarian  relations with his creatures in the  forefront is the controlling theological  issue which sets the range of historical  and contemporary discussions about  dignity and rights in perspective within  the history of Western Christianity  about which alone  I'm modestly competent to speak we can  discern phases in the theoretical and  practical development of positive legal  rights and of natural rights what  precisely these phases are and how they  are related is somewhat disputed  territory among historians theologians  and philosophers and generally speaking  interpretive differences so much to  broader theological and philosophical  positions  my own patchy reading of this history is  shaped by my theological debt to 16th  century English and continental  Reformation thought and practice from  that perspective I am critical of the  eminent conceptuality of human dignity  and subjective rights throughout all  phases of their historical development  but I also wish to discriminate  theoretical and practical articulations  that are more theologically acceptable  with my remaining time therefore let me  briefly sketch a theological account of  human dignity in terms of dynamic divine  human relationships drawing out some  implications for conceptualizing human  rights in this account human dignity  resides pre-eminently in God's manifold  actions by which he makes human beings  in their common existence into finite  images of his infinite perfections by  revealing himself to his human creatures  God communicates his perfections to them  enabling them to participate in his  eternal truth and wisdom his creative  power his bountiful goodness and loving  kindness his righteousness and beauty  God reveals himself to few human beings  in and through the created world which  is the totality of finite creatures in  their ordered relationships and his will  for all human persons is that they come  to know and love themselves their fellow  human beings and the nonhuman creation  has ordered to and by God as imaging his  glory in their distinct modes of being  and action for human beings to know and  to love themselves and their fellow  creatures as manifestations of God's  glory which is their unique dignity is  for them to be continually occupied in a  spiritual worship of  God a worship of reverence adoration  gratitude trust and obedience spiritual  worship generates and gives form not  only to acts of outward worship but to  the whole of the common moral life to  moral freedom and to works of love and  service among fellow human beings human  acts conformed to God's revealed will by  recognizing the specific goodness of all  his creatures but especially of human  beings their unique vocations of  knowledge love and rule over the lower  creatures and the universal and  particular obligations that these impose  in the diverse relationships ordering  their existence within and among  societies God's will revealed in the  created order and immoral acts  conforming to it the right social  relationships preceding these acts and  issuing from them together comprise the  body of natural right of natural justice  worshipful knowledge and practical  obedience are not productions of a self  sufficient human reason and will but of  God's presence too and in the human soul  alumina illuminating the knowing mind  and bringing its spiritual acts to  fruition human beings need God's active  presence not only because of their  natural limitations as psychosomatic  means but because of the sinful  corruption of their minds the deeply  disordered state of their wills and  affections this corruption and disorder  constitutes a wholesale assault on their  dignity precisely because it involves  the redirecting of their affections of  adoration trust and longing away from  God's perfections to an inflated image  of their own so infecting their  knowledge of God of themselves and of  the nonhuman creation  with blind and superstitious fantasies  and depriving them of the moral freedom  to judge and to act rightly it is by  faithful reception of God's saving  revelation in the life death  resurrection and ascension of his  incarnate son that human beings recover  the fullness of their dignity for Jesus  Christ alone has overcome the abyss  between God's communication of his  perfections and humankind's simple  incapacity to respond by bearing the  infirmities of corrupt human nature and  of God's just judgment on human  disobedience to the point of ignominy as  death Jesus manifested simultaneously  the boundlessness of God's forgiving  love for his wayward human creatures and  the perfectly faithful and obedient  human response to God's righteous will  thereby removing the estrangement of  humanity from its maker and breaking its  bondage to sin and death in raising  Jesus from the dead and exalting him to  heavenly glory God decisively recognized  and glorified His image in his human  creature in setting Jesus over the  church to reign to the spirit of  sanctification the father opened the way  for all who are united with Christ  through faith to come into their  inheritance of renewed humanity it is  through the corporate faith and action  of the worshiping church that repentant  sinners participate in an anticipatory  fashion in the human perfections of  their exalted Savior in his freedom and  lordship knowledge and love  righteousness and holiness as fellow  shares in Christ gracious rule through  the Holy Spirit all members of his  spiritual body belong to Christ and to  one another are possessed by Christ and  by one another in  communion of self giving there is no  role in Christ spiritual body for the  work of political or public justice that  is to say the work of rendering coercive  public judgment concerning matters of  communal right and wrong for the  principle purpose of restraining human  wrongdoing and vindicating well-doing  in their ministries to one another and  to those beyond the church's visible  boundaries the faithful stand alongside  their neighbors as equal beneficiaries  of God's saving judgments and not above  their neighbors exercising judgment on  them for the faithful had no judge but  Christ who is both judge and judged  indeed political justice does not  intrinsically and permanently belong to  the perfections of human community  created in and restored to God's image  rather it belongs to the passing age of  sinful humanity and it mirrors both  God's judgment against human evildoing  and his merciful protection of human  community against the destructive forces  of disordered human passions the work of  public justice serves the incomparable  dignity of human beings only by  accepting its limited and remedial  character its dependence on and ordering  to God's creative redeeming and  sanctifying work in human society it  must forego any pretension of affecting  or ensuring the participation of all  persons and communities in the object of  goods and rights of creation and  redemption freely bestowed by the Divine  Spirit and be content with removing the  more damaging external impediments to  such participation  it must renounce the project of defining  social good goods and just relationships  in their royal fullness and be content  to define specific deprivation of good  violations of rights used in the  objective sense harms engine injuries  committed against the individual and the  collective neighbor and degrees of  culpability and to act to correct and  punish such offences its objective must  remain the achievement of peace as  external order the outward harmonization  of wills the visible correction of  wrongdoing and rectification of  injustice the avoidance and termination  of strife and it should not aspire to  encompass the moral regeneration of  offenders and the inward reconciliation  of formerly antagonistic wills let us  now quickly consider some implications  of this account of human dignity natural  right and political justice for  conceptualizing subjective rights it  should be evident that this account does  not require or imply the attribution of  rights to subjects individual or  collective and it is incompatible with  the elaboration of subjective rights  into a groundwork for moral and  political agency action and community  the account places God's active  relations with his human creatures at  the center of the moral and political  universe and attributes Universal and  intrinsic right alone to his self and  will in its act of earthly  manifestations as regards the agency and  action of human beings right refers to  the conformity of their intentions  judgments and actions to the totality of  divinely given goods of persons  communities material and spiritual goods  in their order  and ships within this totality human  beings may encounter one another as  transcending claims as goods that impose  obligations but these obligations are  mediated by their respective  relationships with God earlier versions  of subject of natural light first  propounded by medieval Canon lawyers and  later elaborated by scholastic and neo  Scholastic theologians presented it as a  spiritual faculty power or habit to act  in accordance with objective that to say  natural and divine right or law so for  example the poor man was said to have a  natural right to make use of the surplus  property of the rich to relieve his  extreme need with or without the owner's  permission because he is at de corded  with the divine obligation of sustaining  life very quickly this natural right of  the indigent to act generated a right to  demand or lay claim to the surplus  property of the rich for his use while  the cannabis and scholastics continued  to assert the primacy of objective to  subjective right to their credit they  were also from very early on formulating  a conception of the individual's  ownership of or property in his acts in  other words a conception of freedom as a  property right possessed by the  individual on this proprietary concept  of individual freedom operating within a  progressively attenuated matrix of  natural and divine right were erected  all subsequent contract Aryan theories  of economic social and political society  it took a jurist it  to transpose the paradigm of property  right from the realm of Roman civil and  ecclesiastical law to the realm of  anthropology thereby encouraging a  juridical izing of the world life at its  spiritual root for what is the meaning  purpose theoretical or practical benefit  of a subjective right that is not  defensible in court  it took an equally juristic mind to  suppose that moral obligations entail  subjective rights as their necessary  moral correlate but this is the  prevailing assumption of contemporary  liberalism as we move into an increasing  a juridical and agonistic culture in  which the crusade for egalitarian rights  is hastening the widespread corrosion of  substantive human goods and abetting the  tyrannical aspiration of political  managers to impose the secular  bureaucratic faith and practice on all  social relationships we may well wish to  promote or more theologically open and  less politically circumscribed  conversation among faith about human  dignity and human justice thank you
 

